record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-0-distortion-currency/1 : work {
    title "Phase 6.0: price the encoder's distortion currency against perception, per DCT8x8 frequency"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-conformance/tests/perceptual_frequency.rs",
        path "JPXL/docs/experiments/2026-08-12-distortion-currency-frequency-response.md"
    ]
    intent """
        Phases 5J, 5K, 5L, 5M, 5N and 5O each changed which decision the cover/CfL objective makes while leaving the currency those decisions are priced in untouched, and all six produced scattered, mostly-regressing perceptual results. Before choosing another lever, measure the ruler: how much does one unit of block_cost_bounded's flat coefficient-SSE distortion vary in perceptual cost across the DCT8x8 frequency plane?

        Build a measurement harness that runs no encoder at all. Per XYB channel and per DCT8x8 cell, inject a controlled coefficient error of equal magnitude into every whole block of a real photograph with a deterministic per-block sign, convert back to 8-bit sRGB, and score butteraugli and SSIMULACRA2 against the unperturbed round trip. Equal injected coefficient energy means the current objective charges every cell identically, so the measured spread is exactly the weighting the objective is missing. Report the realized sample-domain SSE per cell as a control against basis-energy artefacts.

        Preregistered: a spread below roughly 1.3x means the flat objective is approximately correct and Phase 6 is abandoned in favour of the queued Phase 5P; a materially larger spread counts only if the shape is also stable across amplitude and across content. Measurement only -- no encoder behaviour changes and no weight table is written, because fitting one to butteraugli's measured response is a separate clean-room decision (AGENTS.md section 2).
        """
    acceptance {
        check harness-measures {
            statement """
                The harness runs against a real photograph, asserts its own self-distance is zero, and reports per-cell butteraugli, SSIMULACRA2 and realized sample-domain SSE for every non-LLF DCT8x8 cell of every XYB channel.
                """
            method command
            command "JPXL_CALIB_REF=<ref.ppm> cargo test --release -p jpxl-conformance --features perceptual --test perceptual_frequency -- --ignored --nocapture"
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-0-frequency-harness-2026-08-12/2 ]
        }
        check spread-decided {
            statement """
                The preregistered flat-versus-structured gate is decided per channel, with the shape's stability across amplitude and across content reported as the qualifying condition, and the result written up as an immutable experiment record.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-0-frequency-spread-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase5o-special8-transform-screen ]
    derived_from [ @jpegxl-rs.observation.phase5-quality-policy-screen-2026-08-11 ]
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.optimization-correctness-gates
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-1-quantizer-normalised-residual/1 : work {
    title "Phase 6.1: re-measure the frequency response per unit of quantizer-normalised error, not raw coefficient error"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-conformance/tests/perceptual_frequency.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/lib.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/quantize.rs"
    ]
    intent """
        Phase 6.0 injected equal *coefficient* error per cell and found a stable 2.65x Y-channel perceptual spread. But real quantization does not inject equal coefficient error: it injects error proportional to that cell's quantization step, and the standard's default dequant matrices (F.3/I.5) are themselves perceptually shaped. So 6.0's spread is an upper bound on the decision-relevant mispricing, not the mispricing itself.

        Re-run the Phase 6.0 harness injecting error proportional to HfQuantizer::step(channel, cell) at the frame's baseline operating point instead of a constant amplitude, using the same corpus, the same amplitude ladder, the same zero-self-distance assertion and the same sample-domain SSE control.

        The two outcomes are sharply different and both are useful. If the response flattens, the standard's matrices already carry the frequency weighting and the objective's only mistake is that it measures error in dequantized rather than quantizer-normalised units -- dividing each cell's squared error by its own step squared is then a small, first-principles, libjxl-independent change with a large effect, and it should be screened on the six-scene matrix. If it does not flatten, the residual is the real target and 6.2/6.3 proceed against that residual rather than against 6.0's raw table.

        Measurement first: no encoder change lands in this work item. If it motivates one, that change is gated separately under the existing Contract B promotion rules.
        """
    acceptance {
        check flatten-decided {
            statement """
                The result explicitly decides whether the standard's default dequant matrices already carry the frequency weighting, and either motivates the quantizer-normalised distortion change as a separately gated work item or redirects 6.2/6.3 onto the measured residual.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-1-flatten-decision-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check residual-measured {
            statement """
                The harness reports a per-cell perceptual response for step-proportional injected error on the same two photographs and amplitude ladder, with the sample-domain SSE control and the zero-self-distance assertion intact.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-1-step-proportional-harness-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-0-distortion-currency ]
    derived_from [ @jpegxl-rs.observation.distortion-currency-misprices-frequency-2026-08-12 ]
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.optimization-correctness-gates
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-2-per-transform-step-shapes/1 : work {
    title "Phase 6.2: per-transform step shapes, so a frequency weight can be applied across transform sizes"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-conformance/tests/perceptual_frequency.rs" ]
    intent """
        Phases 6.0 and 6.1 measured DCT8x8 only. block_cost_bounded's whole job is comparing a large square transform against four sub-quadrants, so a weight defined only for 8x8 cannot be wired into it: applying an 8x8 table to a cross-size comparison would introduce a new bias rather than remove the existing one. This work item is the prerequisite that makes the Phase 6.3 encoder change safe, and it must complete before 6.3 lands.

        Extend the harness to DCT16x16 and DCT32x32: generalise the injection loop off the hard-coded SIDE/CELLS constants to a transform's own coefficient grid, use the matching jpxl_core::dct inverse and the matching I.2.5 default dequant matrix, and run both the Phase 6.0 (equal coefficient error) and Phase 6.1 (step-proportional) sweeps for each size on the same corpus and amplitude ladder.

        The question is not just each size's own shape but whether the three are consistent under a single frequency parameterisation. Report each size's normalised weight against normalised spatial frequency rather than raw cell index, so a 16x16 cell at half-Nyquist can be compared with the 8x8 cell at half-Nyquist. If the three collapse onto one curve, a single per-transform table derived from the standard's matrices is sound and 6.3 can proceed. If they do not, say so: a weight that is right for 8x8 and wrong for 32x32 would bias cover selection toward or against large transforms, which is exactly the decision this track is trying to fix.

        Also report, per size, the sample-domain SSE control and the ratio of the size's own step shape, since the larger transforms have wider grids and may reach further outside the linear regime -- the same effect that made Phase 6.1's chroma numbers unusable.

        Measurement only: no encoder change lands here.
        """
    acceptance {
        check cross-size-consistency-decided {
            statement """
                The three sizes' normalised weights are reported against normalised spatial frequency and the record states explicitly whether they collapse onto one curve, and therefore whether a per-transform weight can be applied to cover selection without biasing the cross-size comparison.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-2-cross-size-decision-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check sizes-measured {
            statement """
                Both the equal-coefficient-error and step-proportional sweeps run for DCT8x8, DCT16x16 and DCT32x32 on the same corpus and amplitude ladder, each with its own inverse transform, its own I.2.5 default matrix, the zero-self-distance assertion and the sample-domain SSE control.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-2-cross-size-harness-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-1-quantizer-normalised-residual ]
    derived_from [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.quantizer-normalised-distortion-is-the-lever-2026-08-12
    ]
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.optimization-correctness-gates
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-2-per-transform-step-shapes/2 : work {
    title "Phase 6.2: per-transform step shapes, so a frequency weight can be applied across transform sizes"
    state completed
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-conformance/tests/perceptual_frequency.rs" ]
    intent """
        Phases 6.0 and 6.1 measured DCT8x8 only. block_cost_bounded's whole job is comparing a large square transform against four sub-quadrants, so a weight defined only for 8x8 cannot be wired into it: applying an 8x8 table to a cross-size comparison would introduce a new bias rather than remove the existing one. This work item is the prerequisite that makes the Phase 6.3 encoder change safe, and it must complete before 6.3 lands.

        Extend the harness to DCT16x16 and DCT32x32: generalise the injection loop off the hard-coded SIDE/CELLS constants to a transform's own coefficient grid, use the matching jpxl_core::dct inverse and the matching I.2.5 default dequant matrix, and run both the Phase 6.0 (equal coefficient error) and Phase 6.1 (step-proportional) sweeps for each size on the same corpus and amplitude ladder.

        The question is not just each size's own shape but whether the three are consistent under a single frequency parameterisation. Report each size's normalised weight against normalised spatial frequency rather than raw cell index, so a 16x16 cell at half-Nyquist can be compared with the 8x8 cell at half-Nyquist. If the three collapse onto one curve, a single per-transform table derived from the standard's matrices is sound and 6.3 can proceed. If they do not, say so: a weight that is right for 8x8 and wrong for 32x32 would bias cover selection toward or against large transforms, which is exactly the decision this track is trying to fix.

        Also report, per size, the sample-domain SSE control and the ratio of the size's own step shape, since the larger transforms have wider grids and may reach further outside the linear regime -- the same effect that made Phase 6.1's chroma numbers unusable.

        Measurement only: no encoder change lands here.
        """
    acceptance {
        check cross-size-consistency-decided {
            statement """
                The three sizes' normalised weights are reported against normalised spatial frequency and the record states explicitly whether they collapse onto one curve, and therefore whether a per-transform weight can be applied to cover selection without biasing the cross-size comparison.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-2-cross-size-decision-2026-08-12/2 ]
        }
        check sizes-measured {
            statement """
                Both the equal-coefficient-error and step-proportional sweeps run for DCT8x8, DCT16x16 and DCT32x32 on the same corpus and amplitude ladder, each with its own inverse transform, its own I.2.5 default matrix, the zero-self-distance assertion and the sample-domain SSE control.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-2-cross-size-harness-2026-08-12/2 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-1-quantizer-normalised-residual ]
    derived_from [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.quantizer-normalised-distortion-is-the-lever-2026-08-12
    ]
    implements [
