            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase9-quant-scheduling-speed-2026-08-15/1 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase8-5-leaf-finishing ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-cover-lane-simd/1 : work {
    title "outside-advice.md §8 (S8), narrowed per Opus advisor review: bit-identical coefficient-lane SIMD for cover scoring"
    state completed
    intent """
        sources/outside-advice.md §8 ("Cover selection needs a surrogate-first
        design") asks for an analysis pass + compact per-candidate summaries +
        lower-bound pruning to replace block_cost's exact per-coefficient
        HfQuantizer::choose scoring. Before attempting that, an Opus advisor review
        (requested given the Phase 2 CfL near-misses at a much smaller decision
        surface) was run against the actual machinery (AnalysisAtlas,
        CandidateForwardCache, block_cost_bounded, tile_region, HfQuantizer::choose).
        Verdict: do not attempt the full §8 redesign this session -- a closed-form
        summary score has an unbounded-tail failure mode at cover selection's larger,
        more consequential decision surface (which transform exists, not just a
        correction factor). Recommended instead: a bit-identical coefficient-lane
        SIMD restructuring of block_cost_bounded's inner loop (outside-advice §3's
        "vectorize adjacent coefficients, not one cell's four candidates" -- the
        existing HfQuantizer::choose already has a SIMD path, but on the wrong axis),
        paired with an exact-agreement test harness proving zero divergence, as
        infrastructure that de-risks a later closed-form-scoring attempt without
        gambling on cover-decision correctness now. AnalysisAtlas is explicitly left
        untouched (expanding it now tunes features against a consumer -- the
        summary scorer -- that was deliberately deferred, repeating the atlas's own
        Milestone-1 mistake once already avoided).
        """
    note """
        2026-08-07: landed the recommended narrow slice. Added
        HfQuantizer::choose_lane4 (quantize.rs) -- four adjacent coefficients at
        once via `wide::f32x4`, same zero-threshold shortcut, same
        [0, estimate-1, estimate, estimate+1] candidate order and tie rule, same
        error semantics as four independent choose() calls, plus a whole-lane
        zero fast path (added after measurement showed the naive version -- always
        running the full candidate search even when the scalar zero-threshold
        shortcut would fire -- made cover_ms ~35% *slower* despite fewer choose()
        call-count in diagnostics, since a SIMD lane can't skip per-element work
        the way a scalar early return can). Added a non-simd fallback with the
        same signature (four scalar choose() calls) so callers don't feature-gate.
        Added score_channel_lanes (lib.rs) to drive it from block_cost_bounded:
        row-segment iteration (LLF-prefix skip per row, batches of 4 + scalar
        remainder), accumulating bits/weighted_sse and checking the Phase-1 cutoff
        at cell granularity, in the same raster order as before.

        Verified bit-identical by an exhaustive unit test
        (choose_lane4_is_bit_identical_to_four_scalar_choose_calls, quantize.rs)
        across 6 quantizer configurations (varying transform, HfMul, global_scale,
        qm_scale) and 10 target patterns per lane chosen to stress every boundary
        (zero threshold from both sides, half-integer ties, the |q|<=1 bias-adjust
        branch edge, both sides of MAX_QUANT), and by --diag fingerprint identity
        on real images (12MP: output_bytes/fingerprint 1,530,188 /
        30e55ba3faef4d64, unchanged). cover_ms improved from ~1784ms to a stable
        ~1524-1532ms (~14-15%) on the 12MP image after adding the whole-lane zero
        fast path. Full test suite green modulo the pre-existing, unrelated
        both_oracles_decode_a_stream_from_the_hf_mul_segment failure (documented
        against jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase2-cfl-quant).

        Deferred, per the advisor's plan: FastDeadZone-in-scoring (a cheaper
        approximate quantizer used only for candidate scoring, gated by this same
        kind of agreement/regret harness generalized to a bounded-regret assertion
        rather than exact equality) and the full §8 closed-form summary redesign
        (needs either richer per-tile statistics than 2nd moments or the
        matched-quality harness outside-advice §19 describes).
        """
    acceptance {
        check bit-identical {
            statement """
                choose_lane4_is_bit_identical_to_four_scalar_choose_calls passes exhaustively across multiple quantizer configs and boundary-stressing target patterns; --diag fingerprint and output_bytes for a fixed 12MP vardct-fixed encode are unchanged.
                """
            method command
            command "cd JPXL && cargo test -p jpxl-encode-policy --lib --release choose_lane4"
            verified_by [
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-cover-lane-simd-postcommit/1,
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-cover-lane-simd-verified/1
            ]
        }
        check measured-faster {
            statement """
                cover_ms on the 12MP vardct-fixed --diag benchmark is not slower than the pre-change baseline (it improved ~14-15% after the whole-lane zero fast path).
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-cover-lane-simd-postcommit/1,
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-cover-lane-simd-verified/1
            ]
        }
        check tests-green {
            statement """
                jpxl-encode-policy lib tests (default and --no-default-features), vardct_roundtrip, vardct_oracle (modulo the pre-existing hf_mul_segment failure) and rate_loop all pass.
                """
            method command
            command "cd JPXL && cargo test -p jpxl-encode-policy --lib --release && cargo test -p jpxl-encode-policy --lib --release --no-default-features && cargo test -p jpxl-encode-policy --test vardct_roundtrip --test vardct_oracle --test rate_loop --release"
            verified_by [
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-cover-lane-simd-postcommit/1,
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-cover-lane-simd-verified/1
            ]
        }
    }
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.sources-advice-completion
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.assessment.encoder-architecture-outside-advice ]
    source {
        kind external
        path "sources/outside-advice.md"
        excerpt """
            Section 8: Cover selection needs a surrogate-first design
            """
    }
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-full-redesign-scoped/1 : work {
    title "S8 full redesign, scoped: infrastructure + measurement + one conservative prune (not \"full §8 landed\")"
    state superseded
    intent """
        Scope (not implement) the full outside-advice.md §8 surrogate-first
        cover-selection redesign, per explicit user request, via two parallel
        Explore scoping agents (architecture/machinery; safety/verification/
        prerequisites) followed by an Opus advisor synthesis, each independently
        checked against the actual code rather than accepted at face value.

        Headline finding governing the whole plan: §8's core premise -- "avoid
        forward-transforming losing candidates via cheap summaries" -- is mostly
        foreclosed. Ranking a DCT16/DCT32 candidate needs coefficient-domain data
        (a DCT32 spans 16 atoms; per-atom source mean/variance cannot recover its
        spectrum), so a useful summary requires the forward DCT to already exist --
        the forward is unavoidable. The achievable win shrinks to "skip the exact
        per-cell choose/choose_lane4 scoring loop for losers after the transform
        already ran," and that win is currently unmeasured.

        Second finding: rate (residual_bits(q), discrete/non-smooth) cannot be
        tightly bounded from a summary without approaching per-cell data, but a
        loose PROVABLE bound exists (every guaranteed-nonzero cell costs >=2 bits)
        and distortion CAN be tightly/exactly bounded for cells guaranteed to zero
        (weighted_sse is a smooth quadratic, computable from coefficient magnitude
        alone). So a provable -- not merely empirically-usually-right -- lower
        bound is achievable, just looser than the existing exact partial-sum cutoff
        in block_cost_bounded's `check`.

        Given both findings, the honest deliverable is infrastructure + measurement
        + one conservative provably-safe pruning application, not "the full §8
        redesign landed." The aggressive closed-form rate-estimate half of §8 is
        explicitly staged out (Phase F) pending a regret ceiling from real
        measurement -- attempting it now repeats jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase2-cfl-quant's
        failure (two reverted closed-form-estimate regressions from 3
        sufficient-statistics) at cover selection's larger, more consequential
        decision surface (which transform exists, not a correction factor).

        Full phase breakdown (six phases, each independently gated) is recorded in
        the local plan file used to produce this record
        (~/.claude/plans/continue-in-this-folder-breezy-chipmunk.md at scoping
        time) and summarized in the note below since AKR is the durable source of
        record per AGENTS.md, not the local plan file.
        """
    note """
        Phase A -- COMPLETE, see jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-phase-a-regret-harness.
        crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/regret.rs: CoverSurrogate trait, ExactPolicy,
        RegretSample/RegretSummary (agreement_rate, mean/max/p99 regret), and an
        independent measure_region walk (never touches tile_region/the production
        path; always computes unbounded exact costs). Exit gate met: 0 regret /
        100% agreement wiring the exact scorer in as its own surrogate, PLUS a
        stronger cross-validation against tile_region's actual production decisions
        (not just self-consistency) so the twin implementations can't silently
        drift. Zero production-path impact confirmed via --diag fingerprint
        identity. Original phase text preserved below for the rest of the plan.

        Phase A (as planned) -- regret harness (agreement rate + exact-cost regret
        with TAIL reporting, not just mean, since both CfL regressions were rare/
        systematic wrong picks not average drift), using the exact scorer
        (block_cost/block_cost_bounded) as its own ground truth -- zero new
        dependencies, no Butteraugli needed for the cover-DECISION question
        (perceptual validation of the objective itself is separate Phase-5
        territory). Exit gate: proven as a no-op first (exact scorer as both
        surrogate and oracle -> 0 regret, 100% agreement), mirroring how
        choose_lane4's bit-identity test was proven against itself before being
        trusted (quantize.rs:870).

        Phase B (NEXT) -- two measurements that can kill or reshape the plan, run
        BEFORE any summary machinery is built: (1) choose-loop cost share vs
        forward-DCT cost share in cover scoring (kill criterion if too small, e.g.
        <~15%); (2) cross-probe WINNER stability (currently unverified -- the
        measured 96-97% cross-probe cache hit rate from
        jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase3-forward-cache only covers the candidate SET
        being probe-invariant, not the winning transform per region). If winners
        churn across probes, the memory-saving "cache summaries not coefficients"
        design must be dropped in favor of scoring-loop-only pruning with the
        full-coefficient cache kept as-is.

        Phase C -- compact per-candidate summary (per-band magnitude
        histogram/counts; respects Y-scored-first -> B-target coupling,
        lib.rs:2078,2088), implementing the combined provable lower bound
        (distortion floor + rate floor). Validated EXHAUSTIVELY (not statistically
        -- it's a provable bound, so any pruned-but-actually-winning candidate is a
        bug) against the exact scorer over the corpus.

        Phase D -- wire the safe prune behind a feature flag, survivors still
        scored exactly (block_cost_bounded unchanged) so winners' bits/sse stay
        exact. vardct_oracle.rs's external-decoder tests prove PLACEMENT
        conformance only, not decision quality -- a legal-but-worse pick sails
        through untouched, the same gap that let both CfL regressions through
        until a bespoke fixture caught them. New decision-quality fixtures
        (same class as the CfL-catching ones) required, driven by the Phase-A
        harness with an explicit tail-regret budget. Honest negative result
        (flag stays off) is an acceptable outcome if Phase B's predicted win
        doesn't materialize.

        Phase E -- test-set/ has no provenance sidecars (AGENTS.md §9 violation,
        "fixtures without provenance do not merge") and isn't stratified per
        outside-advice §19; required before Phase D's fixtures can merge.

        Phase F -- explicitly NOT attempted: the tight, continuous, closed-form
        rate estimate (§8's ambitious half). Gated on Phase A live + Phase D's
        measured tail-regret ceiling; named explicitly so it isn't silently
        smuggled into an earlier phase under time pressure.

        Out of scope for the whole plan: AnalysisAtlas expansion (source-pixel
        per-atom stats can't rank DCT16/32; its raw-frame.xyb() vs
        Gaborish-preconditioned-planes mismatch is a separate real bug, logged
        separately, not fixed here); Butteraugli/SSIMULACRA2 build-out (Phase 5
        territory); "full §8 redesign landed" as one deliverable.

        See also (precedent/context, referenced in prose since `supported_by`
        cannot target `work` records): jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-cover-lane-simd (the
        prior narrow slice and its advisor reasoning) and
        jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase2-cfl-quant (the CfL failure this plan avoids
        repeating).
        """
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.sources-advice-completion
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.assessment.encoder-architecture-outside-advice ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-full-redesign-scoped/2 : work {
    title "S8 full redesign, scoped: infrastructure + measurement + one conservative prune (not \"full §8 landed\")"
    state superseded
    intent """
        Scope (not implement) the full outside-advice.md §8 surrogate-first
        cover-selection redesign, per explicit user request, via two parallel
        Explore scoping agents (architecture/machinery; safety/verification/
        prerequisites) followed by an Opus advisor synthesis, each independently
        checked against the actual code rather than accepted at face value.

        Headline finding governing the whole plan: §8's core premise -- "avoid
        forward-transforming losing candidates via cheap summaries" -- is mostly
        foreclosed. Ranking a DCT16/DCT32 candidate needs coefficient-domain data
        (a DCT32 spans 16 atoms; per-atom source mean/variance cannot recover its
        spectrum), so a useful summary requires the forward DCT to already exist --
        the forward is unavoidable. The achievable win shrinks to "skip the exact
        per-cell choose/choose_lane4 scoring loop for losers after the transform
        already ran."

        Second finding: rate (residual_bits(q), discrete/non-smooth) cannot be
        tightly bounded from a summary without approaching per-cell data, but a
        loose PROVABLE bound exists (every guaranteed-nonzero cell costs >=2 bits)
        and distortion CAN be tightly/exactly bounded for cells guaranteed to zero
        (weighted_sse is a smooth quadratic, computable from coefficient magnitude
        alone). So a provable -- not merely empirically-usually-right -- lower
        bound is achievable, just looser than the existing exact partial-sum cutoff
        in block_cost_bounded's `check`.

        Third finding (Phase B, see note): CandidateForwardCache is keyed on
        (transform, px, py) only -- forwards are probe-invariant by construction,
        already getting 96-97% cross-probe reuse for free, and are the dominant
        65-69% of cover-scoring cost. This forecloses §8's memory-saving half
        entirely (discarding them to save memory would forfeit free reuse and
        re-pay the dominant cost) independent of the cross-probe winner-churn
        question Phase B also measured.

        Given all three findings, the honest deliverable is infrastructure +
        measurement + one conservative, provably-safe, WITHIN-PROBE-ONLY pruning
        application that never touches the forward cache -- not "the full §8
        redesign landed" and not the memory-saving design §8 originally proposed.
        The aggressive closed-form rate-estimate half of §8 is explicitly staged
        out (Phase F) pending a regret ceiling from real measurement -- attempting
        it now repeats jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase2-cfl-quant's failure (two reverted
        closed-form-estimate regressions from 3 sufficient-statistics) at cover
        selection's larger, more consequential decision surface.

        Full phase breakdown (originally six phases) is recorded in the local plan
        file used to produce this record
        (~/.claude/plans/continue-in-this-folder-breezy-chipmunk.md at scoping
        time) and kept current in the note below since AKR is the durable source of
        record per AGENTS.md, not the local plan file.
        """
    note """
        Phase A -- COMPLETE, see jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-phase-a-regret-harness.
        Regret/agreement harness (crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/regret.rs), proven
        as a no-op and cross-validated against tile_region's real decisions.

        Phase B -- COMPLETE, see jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-phase-b-measurements. Both
        measurements landed (diagnostics.rs StageTimer::CoverForward/CoverScore;
        new stability.rs measure_winner_stability at ATOM granularity, the correct
        comparison unit confirmed by the advisor since varblock origins aren't
        comparable when the quadtree decomposition itself differs across probes).
        Results: choose-loop score share 31-35% of cover time (passes the ~15%
        gate, but is an UPPER BOUND on the prune's win -- only losers get skipped,
        winners still score exactly, and the dominant forward cost is paid
        regardless; realistic win is low-teens percent of cover time, a modest
        single-digit encode-time improvement, not transformative). Winner churn:
        mean 9.4%, near-zero (14/33 pairs exactly 0.0000) during monotonic
        bisection convergence, concentrated in 4 pairs at the initial geometric
        bracket phase and the Fast->Full refinement handoff; one unexplained
        97.27% spike at a tiny 32-rung step inside the converged region (i=9,
        hypothesized but unverified synthetic-fixture artifact -- see Phase E).

        ADVISOR VERDICT (decisive on its own, independent of the churn pattern):
        CandidateForwardCache keys on (transform, px, py) only -- forwards are
        PROBE-INVARIANT, already get 96-97% cross-probe reuse for free, and are
        the dominant cost. Two things this plan had conflated: "the prune" (skip
        choose-loop for losers, within-probe, touches the cache not at all) versus
        "the memory-discard design" (§8's original cache-summaries-not-
        coefficients idea, the ONLY thing churn bears on). Given probe-invariant,
        dominant, already-free-to-reuse forwards, discarding them is a strictly
        bad trade regardless of churn -- DROP the memory-discard design outright.
        Do NOT phase-gate the prune to the stable-convergence region either (a
        suggestion from this session the advisor called a category error) -- the
        prune can't hurt cross-probe reuse in any phase since it never touches the
        cache, so run it always.

        Phase C -- COMPLETE, see jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-phase-c-prune-bound.
        Landed the transient provable lower bound the advisor's Phase B revision
        called for: crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/quantize.rs gained
        HfQuantizer::cell_lower_bound (exact distortion floor for guaranteed-zero
        cells, loose-but-provable >=2-bit rate floor for guaranteed-nonzero cells,
        same error semantics as choose); crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/regret.rs
        gained validate_candidate_prune/measure_prune_safety, a second independent
        quadtree walk staging the bound exactly as block_cost_bounded's Y-then-
        X-then-B running-total order/units would (B's cheap check uses Y's REAL
        reconstruction, never raw cb, since kB=1.0 makes that coupling
        safety-load-bearing -- kX=0.0 needs no such care). Validated two ways:
        (1) exhaustive property test in quantize.rs, 6 quantizer configs x 12
        boundary targets, bits_lb<=bits_exact and sse_lb<=sse_exact+1e-9 in every
        case; (2) regret.rs's PruneSummary over an independent quadtree walk,
        safety_violations=0 asserted as the exit gate, prune_rate recorded
        (0.10 on the Phase C fixture) as the separate usefulness measurement, not
        asserted against a threshold (that was Phase D's decision). Purely
        additive: lib.rs (the production cover-scoring path) is untouched by this
        phase -- no --diag byte-identity check applies, unlike Phase B's
        instrumentation which did wrap production call sites. Evidence:
        jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-c-prune-bound-verified.

        Phase D -- COMPLETE, see jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-phase-d-prune-wired. Wired
        Phase C's bound into block_cost_bounded's real Y-then-X-then-B
        cutoff-bounded calls, behind a new `s8-cover-prune` Cargo feature NOT in
        default (flag off compiles byte-identical to before this phase). Added
        cheap_stage_would_prune (lib.rs) and diagnostics counters
        (stage_cover_prune_ns, cover_prune_checks, cover_prune_hits). Decision
        quality proved TWO ways: (1) regret.rs's wired_prune_does_not_change_-
        tile_regions_decisions (s8-cover-prune-only test) -- tile_region's real
        total cost with the prune live matches the harness's independent
        never-pruned ground truth to 1e-6, over ramp_frame and a new noisy_frame
        (gradient+hash-noise, more guaranteed-nonzero cells); (2) real-content
        byte-identity via `jpxl bench vardct-rate --diag`, flag off vs on:
        IDENTICAL fingerprint and output_bytes on both a synthetic 512x512 frame
        (fingerprint=cf22c12684ce773a, prune_checks=3072 hits=277, ~9.0% prune
        rate) and a real 1024x768 photo (fingerprint=576ebe32464ae23a,
        prune_checks=7943 hits=765, ~9.6% prune rate, matching Phase C's ~10%).
        The wired prune changes zero bits of encoder output on real content, not
        just synthetic unit-test fixtures.

        HONEST NEGATIVE RESULT on speed, the exit gate's hardest clause: cover_ms
        INCREASED with the flag on in both measurements (synthetic 13.1->15.7ms,
        +20%; real photo 36.9->46.3ms, +25%). cover_score_ms did drop slightly
        (the skipped exact loops are real: 11.9->10.8ms synthetic, 31.9->30.8ms
        photo) but cover_prune_ms's own cost exceeds that saving (3.4ms synthetic,
        10.5ms photo). Likely cause: cell_lower_bound's per-cell loop is scalar,
        while the exact loop it sometimes replaces is SIMD-batched via
        choose_lane4 (4 cells/call) -- and choose already has its own
        zero-threshold fast path for the guaranteed-zero case (the same one
        cell_lower_bound reuses), so a guaranteed-zero cell was already cheap.
        Checking every candidate at all three stages to catch the ~9-10% that
        prune costs more than it saves. Total wall time barely moves (cover
        scoring is a small slice of total encode time), but the mechanism itself
        is a net loss in isolation -- an acceptable, plan-sanctioned honest
        negative result, not a bug to chase.

        DECISION: infrastructure lands (proven safe + decision-preserving by both
        Phase C's exhaustive proof and this phase's real-content measurement), but
        `s8-cover-prune` is NOT promoted to default and should not be turned on.
        A future fix, if pursued, is architectural (batch cell_lower_bound the way
        choose_lane4 batches choose, or check only at the cheapest-relative-to-
        remaining stage) -- not a reason to revisit the safety proof. Evidence:
        jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-d-prune-wired-verified.

        Phase E -- test-set/ has no provenance sidecars (AGENTS.md §9 violation)
        and isn't stratified per outside-advice §19. Phase D introduced no NEW
        fixtures (reused ramp_frame/noisy_frame-style synthetic generators and an
        existing .agent/scratch/ photo already used by a prior real-world bench
        run), so Phase E's original trigger ("required before Phase D's fixtures
        can merge") may not fire -- re-assess before starting E. The advisor's
        added natural-photo re-run of the Phase-B i=9 winner-churn anomaly is
        still open and independently worth doing regardless of Phase D's outcome.

        Phase F -- explicitly NOT attempted: the tight, continuous, closed-form
        rate estimate (§8's ambitious half). Was gated on Phase D's measured
        tail-regret ceiling; Phase D's speed result was negative, so Phase F's
        justification (extend a within-probe prune that didn't pay for itself) is
        now weaker than when this plan was scoped -- if pursued at all, needs its
        own fresh justification, not inheritance from Phase D's infrastructure.

        Out of scope for the whole plan: AnalysisAtlas expansion; §8's
        memory-discard/cache-summaries design (DROPPED at Phase B, see above, not
        just deferred); Butteraugli/SSIMULACRA2 build-out (Phase 5 territory);
        "full §8 redesign landed" as one deliverable.

        See also (precedent/context, referenced in prose since `supported_by`
        cannot target `work` records): jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-cover-lane-simd,
        jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase2-cfl-quant, jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase3-forward-cache
        (established the 96-97% candidate-set cross-probe reuse this phase built
        on), jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-phase-a-regret-harness,
        jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-phase-b-measurements,
        jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-phase-c-prune-bound,
        jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-phase-d-prune-wired.
        """
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.sources-advice-completion
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
    supersedes [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-full-redesign-scoped/1 ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.assessment.encoder-architecture-outside-advice ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-full-redesign-scoped/3 : work {
    title "S8 full redesign, scoped: infrastructure + measurement + one conservative prune (not \"full §8 landed\")"
    state completed
    intent """
        Scope (not implement) the full outside-advice.md §8 surrogate-first
        cover-selection redesign, per explicit user request, via two parallel
        Explore scoping agents (architecture/machinery; safety/verification/
        prerequisites) followed by an Opus advisor synthesis, each independently
        checked against the actual code rather than accepted at face value.

        Headline finding governing the whole plan: §8's core premise -- "avoid
        forward-transforming losing candidates via cheap summaries" -- is mostly
        foreclosed. Ranking a DCT16/DCT32 candidate needs coefficient-domain data
        (a DCT32 spans 16 atoms; per-atom source mean/variance cannot recover its
        spectrum), so a useful summary requires the forward DCT to already exist --
        the forward is unavoidable. The achievable win shrinks to "skip the exact
        per-cell choose/choose_lane4 scoring loop for losers after the transform
        already ran."

        Second finding: rate (residual_bits(q), discrete/non-smooth) cannot be
        tightly bounded from a summary without approaching per-cell data, but a
        loose PROVABLE bound exists (every guaranteed-nonzero cell costs >=2 bits)
        and distortion CAN be tightly/exactly bounded for cells guaranteed to zero
        (weighted_sse is a smooth quadratic, computable from coefficient magnitude
        alone). So a provable -- not merely empirically-usually-right -- lower
        bound is achievable, just looser than the existing exact partial-sum cutoff
        in block_cost_bounded's `check`.

        Third finding (Phase B, see note): CandidateForwardCache is keyed on
        (transform, px, py) only -- forwards are probe-invariant by construction,
        already getting 96-97% cross-probe reuse for free, and are the dominant
        65-69% of cover-scoring cost. This forecloses §8's memory-saving half
        entirely (discarding them to save memory would forfeit free reuse and
        re-pay the dominant cost) independent of the cross-probe winner-churn
        question Phase B also measured.

        Given all three findings, the honest deliverable is infrastructure +
        measurement + one conservative, provably-safe, WITHIN-PROBE-ONLY pruning
        application that never touches the forward cache -- not "the full §8
        redesign landed" and not the memory-saving design §8 originally proposed.
        The aggressive closed-form rate-estimate half of §8 is explicitly staged
        out (Phase F) pending a regret ceiling from real measurement -- attempting
        it now repeats jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase2-cfl-quant's failure (two reverted
        closed-form-estimate regressions from 3 sufficient-statistics) at cover
        selection's larger, more consequential decision surface.

        Full phase breakdown (originally six phases) is recorded in the local plan
        file used to produce this record
        (~/.claude/plans/continue-in-this-folder-breezy-chipmunk.md at scoping
        time) and kept current in the note below since AKR is the durable source of
        record per AGENTS.md, not the local plan file.
        """
    note """
        CLOSED 2026-08-08. Code review of Phase C/D (crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/{lib,regret,quantize,diagnostics}.rs) found zero correctness bugs -- the prune primitive and its wiring are provably safe and correctly gated off by default. Two cleanup findings: (1) diagnostics.rs's Phase D counters/StageTimer::CoverPrune/summary_line segment were unconditionally compiled despite their only callers being feature-gated -- FIXED, now #[cfg(feature = "s8-cover-prune")] throughout, verified both configs build/test clean (65/65 default, 66/66 with feature), see jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-d-diagnostics-gated-verified; (2) regret.rs's ~400-line Phase C prune-safety harness is arguably redundant now that Phase D's wired test directly proves the production path -- KEPT AS-IS (soft call): Phase A's measure_region/RegretSample/ExactPolicy and Phase D's wired_prune_does_not_change_tile_regions_decisions are not redundant (the latter is the production-path proof, the former its ground-truth walk), and Phase C's measure_prune_safety proves the bound *primitive's* safety independent of wiring -- cheap insurance for whoever later batches cell_lower_bound the way choose_lane4 batches choose.

        Opus advisor reorientation (independently verified against code+ledger, not just this record's framing): Phase F (S8's aggressive closed-form rate-estimate half) is a FIRM NO-GO -- its own within-probe predecessor (Phase D) was a net loss, the realistic ceiling is now ~3% of VarDCT wall (cover is 1.79s of an 8.3s 12MP total post-Phase-1-3, entropy at 3.68s is now the fat stage), and CfL's closed-form estimate already failed the same way twice at a smaller/safer surface (see jpegxl-rs.evidence.* CfL revert precedent). Next round of optimization work is reoriented to Phase 4 (modular planning rebuild, outside-advice.md §14-16), reframed density-first since Phase 1 already fixed modular's cheap copy/interpretation problems (Arc-shared score planes, specialized single-leaf kernels, cheap-vs-exact trial pricing) -- what remains is model-strength: no Weighted predictor, local-only trees, full-image trial scans, naive LZ77 lookback. Justified by modular being ~4x VarDCT's wall and the least-addressed gap (~22% wall reduction from 1 phase vs VarDCT's ~45% from 3), plus the safest major rebuild available since lossless gives an exact-pixel oracle with no perceptual-calibration dependency. Scoping handed off to jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase4-modular-scoped.
        """
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.sources-advice-completion
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
    supersedes [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-full-redesign-scoped/2 ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.assessment.encoder-architecture-outside-advice ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-phase-a-regret-harness/1 : work {
    title "S8 Phase A: regret/agreement harness, proven as a no-op against the exact scorer"
    state completed
    intent """
        Phase A of jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-full-redesign-scoped: build a
        regret/agreement harness comparing a pluggable cover-selection "surrogate"
        policy against the exact scorer (block_cost/block_cost_bounded) as ground
        truth, reporting agreement rate and exact-cost regret (with tail
        reporting -- max/p99, not just mean, since both jpegxl-rs.work.arch-
        phase2-cfl-quant regressions were rare/systematic wrong picks, not average
        drift). Zero new dependencies; no Butteraugli needed for the cover-DECISION
        question (the exact scorer is its own ground truth for "does a surrogate
        reproduce the current objective", a separate question from whether that
        objective tracks perception, which is Phase-5 territory).

        Exit gate: prove the harness as a no-op first -- wire the exact scorer in
        as both surrogate and oracle, assert 0 regret / 100% agreement -- before it
        is trusted for anything else, mirroring how choose_lane4's bit-identity
        test was proven against itself (quantize.rs:870) before the S8 SIMD slice
        was trusted.
        """
    note """
        2026-08-07: landed. New module crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/regret.rs:
        CoverSurrogate trait (decide(split_cost, single_cost: Option<f64>) ->
        Split|Merge), ExactPolicy (wraps tile_region's own tie rule), RegretSample/
        RegretSummary (agreement_rate, mean/max/p99 regret), and measure_region --
        an INDEPENDENT recursive quadtree walk (deliberately not touching
        tile_region or the production encode path at all) that always computes the
        UNBOUNDED exact merge cost at every node (never applying tile_region's own
        Phase-1 partial-sum cutoff), because ground truth for regret needs a true
        cost, not a bound.

        Two tests: (1) exact_policy_is_a_true_no_op -- the Phase-A exit gate,
        asserts 0 regret / 100% agreement wiring the exact scorer in as its own
        surrogate; (2) measure_region_matches_tile_regions_own_total_cost -- a
        stronger check than the plan asked for: cross-validates this module's
        independent walk against tile_region's ACTUAL production decisions (not
        just self-consistency), so the twin implementations cannot silently drift
        apart. Both pass.

        Verified additive/zero-risk by construction and by measurement:
        --diag fingerprint/byte identity on the 12MP image unchanged
        (1,530,188 / 30e55ba3faef4d64) after adding this module, confirming it has
        no effect on the production encode path (expected, since it's a read-only
        parallel walk never invoked by tile_region/select_blocks/plan_at). Full
        jpxl-encode-policy lib suite 62/62 (was 60, +2 new tests), vardct_roundtrip
        12/12.

        Next: Phase B (jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-full-redesign-scoped's plan) -- two
        measurements before building any summary machinery: choose-loop cost share
        vs forward-DCT cost share in cover scoring, and cross-probe WINNER
        stability (currently unverified, load-bearing for whether the
        memory-saving cache-summaries-not-coefficients design survives).
        """
    acceptance {
        check no-drift {
            statement """
                measure_region_matches_tile_regions_own_total_cost passes: the harness's independent quadtree walk reaches the same total exact cost as tile_region's own production decisions on the same inputs.
                """
            method command
            command "cd JPXL && cargo test -p jpxl-encode-policy --lib --release regret"
            verified_by [
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-a-regret-harness-postcommit/1,
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-a-regret-harness-verified/1
            ]
        }
        check no-op-proven {
            statement """
                exact_policy_is_a_true_no_op passes: wiring the exact scorer in as both surrogate and oracle yields 0 regret and 100% agreement at every measured node.
                """
            method command
            command "cd JPXL && cargo test -p jpxl-encode-policy --lib --release regret"
            verified_by [
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-a-regret-harness-postcommit/1,
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-a-regret-harness-verified/1
            ]
        }
        check zero-production-impact {
            statement """
                The harness is additive-only: --diag fingerprint and output_bytes for a fixed 12MP vardct-fixed encode are unchanged after adding it, and the full jpxl-encode-policy lib and vardct_roundtrip suites stay green.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-a-regret-harness-postcommit/1,
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-a-regret-harness-verified/1
            ]
        }
    }
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.sources-advice-completion
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.assessment.encoder-architecture-outside-advice ]
    source {
        kind external
        path "sources/outside-advice.md"
        excerpt """
            Section 8: Cover selection needs a surrogate-first design
            """
    }
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-phase-b-measurements/1 : work {
    title "S8 Phase B: choose-loop cost share + cross-probe winner stability measured; memory-discard design dropped"
    state completed
    intent """
        Phase B of jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-full-redesign-scoped: two
        measurements that can kill or reshape the rest of the plan, run before any
        summary machinery is built. (1) choose-loop cost share vs forward-DCT cost
        share in cover scoring, kill criterion ~15%. (2) cross-probe WINNER
        stability (not just candidate-set stability, already measured at 96-97% by
        jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase3-forward-cache) -- unverified and load-bearing for
        whether a "cache summaries, not coefficients" memory-saving redesign can
        preserve cross-probe cache reuse.
        """
    note """
        2026-08-07: both measurements landed and an Opus advisor consulted with
        the real data (same advisor session as the full-plan synthesis).

        Measurement 1 (crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/diagnostics.rs,
        StageTimer::CoverForward/CoverScore wrapping block_cost_bounded's
        cache.get_or_insert call and score_channel_lanes calls respectively; pure
        instrumentation, --diag byte-identity confirmed unchanged): choose-loop
        scoring is 31-35% of (forward+score) cover time, stable across a 12MP and a
        1024x768 image, 3 repeated runs. Well above the ~15% kill threshold.

        Measurement 2 (new crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/stability.rs,
        measure_winner_stability: drives a real rate::search_frame, replays every
        distinct probed quantizer through plan_at_on sharing one
        CandidateForwardCache exactly as the real rate loop does, compares
        consecutive probes' covers at ATOM granularity -- not varblock origins,
        which aren't comparable across probes when the quadtree decomposition
        itself differs; an atom always belongs to exactly one varblock in every
        probe regardless of decomposition, so atom-to-transform is the correct
        invariant comparison unit, confirmed correct by the advisor): 33 distinct
        probes on a 256x256 synthetic image, mean_adjacent_churn=9.4%,
        max_adjacent_churn=97.3%, first_vs_last_churn=99.2%. The aggregate hides a
        clean pattern in the per-pair sequence: churn is exactly 0.0000 for 14 of
        33 adjacent pairs (two long unbroken runs during monotonic bisection
        convergence) and concentrates almost entirely in 4 pairs -- the initial
        geometric bracket phase (2 pairs, 64.45% each) and the Fast->Full
        refinement handoff (2 pairs, 35.16%/28.12%). One unexplained anomaly: pair
        i=9, a tiny 32-rung step inside the converged bisection region, produced
        the single highest churn in the trace (97.27%) -- hypothesized but NOT
        verified to be a synchronized-flip artifact of the synthetic
        ramp+checkerboard fixture's locally-identical atoms sharing a merge/split
        tie-threshold; flagged as an open note, not asserted as explained.

        ADVISOR VERDICT (decisive, does not hinge on the churn data alone):
        CandidateForwardCache is keyed on ForwardKey(transform, px, py) -- NO
        quantizer/probe component (lib.rs ~967, 989-998). Cached forwards are
        PROBE-INVARIANT by construction, which is exactly why the 96-97% cross-
        probe hit rate already exists for free. Two separate things were conflated
        in the original Phase-C/D sketch: "the prune" (skip the choose-loop for
        losers, a within-probe compute optimization that discards nothing and
        touches cross-probe reuse not at all) versus "the memory-discard design"
        (cache summaries not coefficients, recompute winners' forwards only --
        only THIS is affected by winner churn). Given forwards are probe-invariant
        AND are the dominant 65-69% of cover cost AND already get 96-97% reuse for
        free, discarding them to save memory would forfeit that reuse and re-pay
        the dominant cost -- a strictly bad trade independent of churn. The churn
        data (max 97%, first-vs-last 99%) independently confirms the trade would
        ALSO be risky, but the decision doesn't need it: DROP the memory-discard
        design outright. Keep the full-coefficient cross-probe cache exactly as-is.
        Do NOT phase-gate the prune to the stable-convergence region either (a
        suggestion from this session that the advisor called a category error) --
        the prune can't hurt cross-probe reuse in ANY phase since it never touches
        the cache, so it should just run always.

        Realistic sizing correction: 31-35% score share is an UPPER bound on the
        prune's achievable win, not the win itself -- only LOSERS' score loops get
        skipped (winners are still scored exactly), and the dominant 65-69%
        forward cost is paid by everyone regardless of the prune. Advisor's
        estimate: plausibly low-teens percent of cover time, a modest single-digit
        encode-time win overall. Worth having, not transformative -- record
        honestly, do not oversell.

        DECISION: proceed to Phase C, WITHOUT the memory-discard design. Phase C
        simplifies: the compact per-candidate summary becomes a TRANSIENT structure
        built from the already-cached forward, used only to compute the provable
        lower bound for the prune decision, then dropped -- it does not replace
        what CandidateForwardCache stores. This deletes the "summarize-then-
        discard-coefficients" hazard surface and the cross-probe-winner-stability
        assumption from Phase C/D's critical path entirely. Phase D is unchanged in
        substance (flagged prune, exact scoring for survivors, oracle + Phase-A
        regret-harness verification) but is easier to reason about with no cross-
        probe interaction to worry about.

        Open item for Phase E (corpus work), NOT blocking Phase C: re-run the
        winner-stability measurement on a natural-photo (non-synthetic,
        non-repetitive) image to check whether the i=9 anomaly is a real,
        general phenomenon or an artifact of the synthetic ramp+checkerboard
        fixture's repetitiveness.
        """
    acceptance {
        check advisor-decision-recorded {
            statement """
                Opus advisor consulted with the measured data; decisive verdict recorded: drop the memory-discard ("cache summaries, not coefficients") design given CandidateForwardCache's forwards are probe-invariant and already dominate cover cost with 96-97% free reuse; proceed to Phase C with a transient (not cache-replacing) summary for the within-probe prune only.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-b-measurements-postcommit/1,
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-b-measurements-verified/1
            ]
        }
        check cost-share-measured {
            statement """
                choose-loop scoring cost share of cover scoring measured via new StageTimer::CoverForward/CoverScore diagnostics, confirmed above the ~15% kill threshold (31-35%, stable across two image sizes), with --diag byte-identity confirming the instrumentation is decision-path-neutral.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-b-measurements-postcommit/1,
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-b-measurements-verified/1
            ]
        }
        check winner-stability-measured {
            statement """
                Cross-probe winner churn measured at atom granularity over a real 33-probe rate search; pattern (near-zero during convergence, concentrated at bracket/refinement-handoff phases) recorded alongside the unresolved i=9 anomaly.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-b-measurements-postcommit/1,
                @jpegxl-rs.evidence.s8-phase-b-measurements-verified/1
            ]
        }
    }
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.sources-advice-completion
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.assessment.encoder-architecture-outside-advice ]
    source {
        kind external
        path "sources/outside-advice.md"
        excerpt """
            Section 8: Cover selection needs a surrogate-first design
            """
    }
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-phase-c-prune-bound/1 : work {
    title "S8 Phase C: transient provable lower bound for the within-probe cover-scoring prune"
    state completed
    intent """
        Implement the (advisor-revised, simplified) transient per-candidate lower bound: HfQuantizer::cell_lower_bound gives an exact distortion floor for guaranteed-zero cells and a loose-but-provable >=2-bit rate floor for guaranteed-nonzero cells, staged Y-then-X-then-B (B using Y's real reconstruction, never raw cb, since kB=1.0 makes the coupling load-bearing for safety). Validated two ways: (1) an exhaustive property test in quantize.rs asserting the bound never exceeds the exact per-cell cost across 6 quantizer configs x 12 boundary targets; (2) a second independent quadtree walk in regret.rs (measure_prune_safety/validate_candidate_prune, mirroring measure_region's read-only discipline) that stages the bound exactly as block_cost_bounded's Y/X/B running-total order and units would, and asserts PruneSummary.safety_violations == 0 while recording prune_rate as the usefulness half of the exit gate. Purely additive: lib.rs (the production path) is untouched by this phase; wiring is Phase D's job.
        """
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-full-redesign-scoped ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-phase-d-prune-wired/1 : work {
    title "S8 Phase D: wire the Phase C-proved cheap prune behind a flag — safe, decision-preserving, measured slower"
    state completed
    intent """
        sources/outside-advice.md §8, jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-full-redesign-scoped Phase D. Wire Phase C's staged cheap lower bound (HfQuantizer::cell_lower_bound) into block_cost_bounded's real Y-then-X-then-B cutoff-bounded calls, behind a new Cargo feature `s8-cover-prune` NOT in default (flag off compiles byte-identical to before this phase). Added `cheap_stage_would_prune` (lib.rs), checked at the same three checkpoints tile_region's real merge-candidate cutoff already visits, before that channel's exact choose/choose_lane4 loop runs -- never instead of it, so a survivor is always scored exactly. Added diagnostics counters (stage_cover_prune_ns, cover_prune_checks, cover_prune_hits) to measure the prune's own cost against what it skips. Added a Phase D decision-quality test (regret.rs, s8-cover-prune-only): with the prune actually live inside tile_region's real calls, tile_region's total cost must still exactly match the harness's independent never-pruned ground truth, over two fixtures (ramp_frame, a new noisy_frame with gradient+hash-noise for more guaranteed-nonzero cells) -- 1e-6 tolerance, zero violations. Verified further by real-content byte-identity: `jpxl bench vardct-rate --diag` flag off vs on produced IDENTICAL fingerprint and output_bytes on both a synthetic 512x512 frame (fingerprint=cf22c12684ce773a, cover_prune_checks=3072 hits=277, ~9.0% prune rate) and a real 1024x768 photo (fingerprint=576ebe32464ae23a, cover_prune_checks=7943 hits=765, ~9.6% prune rate) -- the wired prune changes zero bits of encoder output on real content, not just the two unit-test fixtures. HONEST NEGATIVE RESULT on the speed exit gate: cover_ms increased with the flag on in both measurements (synthetic 13.1->15.7ms, +20%; real photo 36.9->46.3ms, +25%) -- cover_score_ms did drop slightly (the skipped exact loops are real) but cover_prune_ms's own cost (scalar per-cell cell_lower_bound calls, vs the SIMD-batched choose_lane4 loop it sometimes replaces, checked at all three Y/X/B stages on 100% of candidates to catch the ~9-10% that prune) exceeds the saving. Total wall time barely moves (cover scoring is a small slice of total encode time), but the mechanism itself is a net loss in isolation. Decision: infrastructure lands, proven safe and decision-preserving by both Phase C's exhaustive proof and this phase's real-content measurement, but `s8-cover-prune` is NOT promoted to default and should not be turned on without an architectural fix (batch cell_lower_bound like choose_lane4 batches choose, or check only at the cheapest-relative-to-remaining stage). Verified: 65/65 lib tests flag off (unchanged), 66/66 flag on; vardct_roundtrip 12/12 both configs; vardct_oracle 9/10 both configs identically (both_oracles_decode_a_stream_from_the_hf_mul_segment fails with the same peak=255 RMSE=119.627 regardless of the flag -- confirmed pre-existing/unrelated in Phase C). cargo build --workspace --release clean. Zero new clippy warnings in either config (diffed against baseline, byte-identical warning sets).
        """
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-s8-full-redesign-scoped ]
}

